WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.070
Bismillahirrahmanirrahim.

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:06.530
Welcome back calculus students. Let's dive
right into it. As we know the product

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:10.630
rule is when you have two functions and you're
trying to get their derivative it's

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.670
going to be the derivative of the first
function times the second plus the

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:20.170
derivative of the second times the
first. Okay so the first

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:25.550
rule I always like to do when we're getting
started is identify which is my f and

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.510
which is my g. For my product and quotient rule
it's usually pretty easy to identify

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.990
which is which because you can tell by the
brackets, right? So here I have my f of x

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:39.370
here and I have my g of x
right here, okay? Now

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:44.850
before we plug in, just like how we did for the
first principles when I was doing

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.990
those examples, I like to first find just my f
prime and my g prime and then plug

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.550
them in, just like how in first principles I
found my f of x plus h and then I plug

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.730
it in. So here, if we find f prime
of x, it will equal

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:04.090
2x using the power rule here and then the
constant rule for derivative is that when

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:08.230
you take the derivative of a constant it goes to
zero. So that's it for my f prime

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.550
and for my g prime of x

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.550
here we're going to take the power rule for both
of these terms. The power rule for

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.390
the first term is, we're going to take that 3
out and then we're going to take the

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.470
power rule of x cubed is going to be 3x squared,
and then we're going to do minus 1

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:28.410
because the power rule of x is 1. We can
expand that. That's going to be

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.450
9x squared minus 1.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:37.930
Now, because we have all our components, we can
plug them into the derivative of the

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.950
product rule. So here, the derivative of p of
x, because it follows the product

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:48.070
rule, is going to be f prime times g
plus g prime times f.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.990
So here f prime is 2x and g is
3x cubed minus x,

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:58.710
plus our f times g prime, f is x squared

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.970
plus 1, and g prime is 9x squared minus 1.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:09.090
We can expand this. This becomes 6x to
the 4 minus 2x squared,

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.530
plus this expansion will be 9x to
the 4 minus x squared.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.310
This will then simplify because of our like
terms. We have two x to the 4 [terms]

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.970
and two x to the 2 [terms]. This
gives us 15x to the 4 minus

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:30.230
3x squared. For our next example, we have y
equals square root of x plus 2

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.610
times x cubed minus x.
Because y takes on the

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.970
same form of f times g, we can
identify both our f and

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:46.210
g and then differentiate using our product rule.
Again, it's really easy to identify

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.790
f and g because they're just separated by
their own independent brackets.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:54.490
And we want to find f prime of x just like we
did for the first example, so we can

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:58.670
just plug those into the formula. But here,
because we have a square root, let's

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.400
first rewrite it as x of a power instead of as
a square root. And we know that

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:08.470
square root of x is the same as x to the half
power or the power of 1 over 2.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.090
and then taking the derivative of just f of x
here, we're going to do power rule on

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:18.150
x to the 1 over 2, which becomes 1 over 2 x to
the minus 1 over 2 because we bring

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.910
that 1 over 2 forward and we subtract 1 from our
half and then 2, once we take the

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.890
derivative of it, it will go to 0 because
constants always go to 0. Just like our

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:31.770
first example, for g prime of x, we can take
that straight away. We don't have to

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:37.090
reorganize or re-represent g of x because

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:41.800
they're both in the proper form. You take our
power rule. Taking the power rule of x

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:46.520
cubed will give us 3x squared, and taking the
power rule of x will give us 1. And

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:51.470
then here, we can plug both of these
into our product rule now

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:56.960
so y prime will equal f prime
of x which is 1

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:02.130
over 2 x to the negative 1 over 2 times g
of x which is x cubed minus x

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:07.470
plus f of x which

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:12.130
is square root of x or let's actually write it
as the power like we did last time

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:17.470
because it's much easier like that x to
the 1 over 2 plus 2 times

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:21.070
g prime which is 3x squared minus 1.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:26.870
Again let's expand these so we can then
simplify and add them like our last

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:32.370
example here don't be worried if you see
weird powers of x because

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:37.790
how it works is we multiply the
coefficients and then we add

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.950
the powers so here we have a coefficient of
half here we have a coefficient of 1,

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:46.110
right, there's [an] arbitrary 1 always, that's
going to be 1 over 2 x, and then we

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:51.540
can add both these powers negative 1
over 2 plus 3 is 5 over

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:56.890
2. and then for the second one we're going
to have minus because this is

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:01.330
1 over 2 times negative 1 or times the negative
that's on the x it's going to be

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:06.830
minus 1 over 2 and we are
going to do negative

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:10.730
1 over 2 plus 1 negative 1 over 2 plus
1 is the same as 1 half

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:16.430
on the other side here we're going to expand
regular binomial expansion here when we

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:21.450
multiply our x to the 1 over 2 times our 3x
squared. The coefficients 1 and 3

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:26.510
multiply will be left with 3. And then 1
over 2 plus 2 is equal to

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:27.570
5 over 2.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:34.090
x to the 1 over 2 times negative 1 will just
be negative x to the 1 over 2.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:41.080
And then here we just have regular expansion 6x
squared minus 2. We can add the x

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:46.550
to the 5 over 2 and the x to the 5 over 2
terms, which will give us 7

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.990
over 2 x to the 5 over 2. We have a
negative 1 over 2 x to the

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:57.330
half and a negative x to the half. This
is going to be minus 3 over 2

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:02.800
x to the half and we have our
plus 6x squared minus

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:03.290
2.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.550
Sometimes in some courses and in some
textbooks they usually like

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.950
to do the x powers in order, so we could
instead write it as 7 over 2 x to the

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:21.170
5 over 2 plus 6x squared minus 3 over 2

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:26.550
x to the 1 over 2 minus 2, because here
x squared is bigger than x

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.030
to the half, obviously.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:30.890
Thank you, and see you in quotient rule.
